# .gitignore.example - An Annotated Guide
#
# This file serves as a detailed, educational version of a typical .gitignore file.
# Comments explain why certain files and directories are ignored.
# You can use this as a reference to build your own project-specific .gitignore.

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: Compiled Source & Binaries
#
# Why: These files are generated from your source code by a compiler or builder.
# They are not the "source of truth" and will be re-created automatically.
# Committing them leads to merge conflicts and bloats the repository.
# ==============================================================================
*.com            # Compiled MS-DOS applications
*.class          # Compiled Java classes
*.dll            # Dynamically linked libraries (often on Windows)
*.exe            # Executable files (often on Windows)
*.o              # Object files (intermediate compiled files)
*.so             # Shared object files (on Linux/Unix)
*.lib            # Static libraries
*.a              # Archive files (often on Unix)
*.pyc            # Compiled Python files
*.swp            # Swap files created by editors like Vim
*.bak            # Backup files often created by editors
*.orig           # Original files saved by tools like `patch`
*.rej            # Rejected patch files
*.swo            # Swap file from Vim

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: Build & Package Output
#
# Why: These directories contain the final output of your build process or packaged
# application. They should be regenerated from source for each build.
# ==============================================================================
build/           # Common build output directory (e.g., for Gradle, Webpack)
dist/            # Distribution folder, common for web projects
target/          # Common for Java (Maven) and Rust projects
.next/           # Build output for Next.js projects
.nuxt/           # Build output for Nuxt.js projects
.svelte-kit/     # Build output for SvelteKit projects
.out/            # Often used for exported static sites (e.g., by Next.js)

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: Dependency Directories
#
# Why: These contain third-party packages managed by a package manager. They can
# be very large and are easily re-installed using a manifest file (like
# package.json, requirements.txt, or Cargo.toml).
# ==============================================================================
node_modules/    # Node.js dependencies
vendor/          # Common for PHP (Composer), Go, and Ruby dependencies
jspm_packages/   # Dependencies from the JSPM package manager
bower_components/# Dependencies from the Bower package manager

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: Log & Cache Files
#
# Why: Logs are environment-specific and generated during runtime. Caches are
# temporary files to speed up processes. Neither should be version controlled.
# ==============================================================================
*.log            # Log files
npm-debug.log*   # NPM debug logs
yarn-debug.log*  # Yarn debug logs
yarn-error.log*  # Yarn error logs
.npm/            # NPM's cache directory
.yarn/           # Yarn's cache and release directory
.pnp/            # Yarn Plug'n'Play directory
.pnp.js          # Yarn Plug'n'Play loader
.eslintcache     # ESLint's cache file
.babelcache/     # Babel's cache directory
.parcel-cache/   # Parcel's cache directory
.vite/           # Vite's cache directory
.rollup.cache    # Rollup's cache file
.nyc_output/     # NYC (Istanbul) code coverage output
coverage/        # Common code coverage output directory
.sass-cache/     # Sass cache directory
.tmp/            # Temporary files directory

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: OS-Generated Files
#
# Why: These files are created by the operating system for its own purposes (like
# indexing or icon display) and have no relevance to the project itself.
# ==============================================================================
.DS_Store        # macOS folder metadata
.DS_Store?       #
._*              # macOS resource forks
.Spotlight-V100  # macOS Spotlight indexing
.Trashes         # macOS trash files
Thumbs.db        # Windows thumbnail cache
ehthumbs.db      # Windows media thumbnail cache
[Dd]esktop.ini   # Windows folder customization file

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: IDE & Editor Configuration
#
# Why: These files store your personal editor settings, which can vary from
# developer to developer. It's better to share project-wide settings via a
# checked-in, non-user-specific file (like .editorconfig).
# ==============================================================================
.idea/           # JetBrains IDEs (IntelliJ, WebStorm, etc.)
.vscode/         # Visual Studio Code (unless you want to share launch/tasks configs)
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/tasks.json
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.sublime-project # Sublime Text project files
*.sublime-workspace # Sublime Text workspace files
.project         # Eclipse project files
.classpath       # Eclipse Java classpath
.settings/       # Eclipse project settings

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: Environment & Sensitive Files
#
# Why: These files contain secrets, API keys, and local configuration that
# should NEVER be committed to version control for security reasons.
# ==============================================================================
.env             # General environment variables
.env.local       # Local environment overrides
.env.*.local     # Environment-specific local overrides (e.g., .env.development.local)
*.pem            # Private key files
*.key            # Private key files
.secrets/        # A directory for secrets
secrets.yml      #
*.kubeconfig     # Kubernetes configuration files

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: Infrastructure & State
#
# Why: Files related to infrastructure-as-code tools that store local state.
# Committing them can cause issues when multiple people run the same tools.
# ==============================================================================
.terraform/      # Terraform local plugins
*.tfstate        # Terraform state file (use remote state instead)
*.tfstate.backup # Terraform state backup
.serverless/     # Serverless framework directory
.vagrant/        # Vagrant local machine state

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: Language & Framework Specific
#
# Why: Various languages and frameworks have their own specific temporary or
# generated files that should be ignored.
# ==============================================================================
# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
env/
venv/
lib/
lib64/

# Rust
*.rs.bk

# Go
*.exe
*.test
*.prof

# Java
*.jar
*.war
*.ear

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: AI Tooling
#
# Why: Local caches, logs, and temporary files generated by AI assistants or
# development tools.
# ==============================================================================
.claude/          # Placeholder for any local cache/files from Claude-related tools
claude_cache/

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: Gemini CLI Specific
# ==============================================================================
.gemini/          # Gemini cache, logs, and temp files
.geminiignore     # Project-specific ignore file for Gemini

# ==============================================================================
# SECTION: Sherlock Reports
#
# Why: These reports contain system-specific file paths and potentially sensitive
# environment details. Ignoring them prevents accidental exposure and mitigates
# risks like path traversal vulnerabilities if the repository is public or shared.
# They are transient reports generated locally.
# ==============================================================================
Sherlock/Report/projects_inventory.csv
Sherlock/Report/projects_summary.csv
